From 1cac54b7dbc575865d3d404255c4e1e49ed3d450 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 07:22:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: choose the output among all the graphics
 devices

Kodi drives one output of one graphics device. OpenDrm() took the first
device that had any output that could be used and never looked at the
others; the order of preference among outputs (the one named in the
settings, an HDMI output whose display takes Dolby Vision Standard, any
HDMI output, the first connected one) only held within that device.

A laptop with two graphics devices has its own panel on the integrated
one and, as a rule, its HDMI socket on the other. With a TV connected
Kodi came up on the panel of the laptop. Seen with an i7-12650H and an
RTX 3080 Ti; a laptop with one device, where panel and HDMI socket are
on the same one, showed Kodi on the TV. Two things were in the way
there, and each alone is enough:

- the integrated device had a display, so the device with the TV was
  not asked;
- the console of the kernel drives the displays of one device only.
  A display on another device is connected and off, and Kodi takes over
  an output that is on (it uses the CRTC that drives it). The kernel
  turns the displays of a device on when the last one to have it open
  closes it (drm_lastclose() restores the console's configuration).

A PC with a display on each of two cards had the same: the first card
won, whatever was connected to the other.

Now, with more than one device that has a primary node:

- every device is opened for the question, its connectors are read the
  way FindConnector() reads them, and it is closed again. If a display
  was found connected and off, all of them are looked at once more: the
  first look, by closing each device, has made the kernel turn it on.
  Without the second look the first start after a boot would show Kodi
  on one display and every later start on another;
- a device that Kodi cannot show itself with is not chosen for what is
  connected to it: one that cannot be asked, one without a render node
  (a USB display, a framebuffer left by the firmware) and, when the
  device is opened for atomic modesetting, one that does not have it.
  Before, such a device could only be taken when it came first; it
  must not be taken now because its display ranks higher;
- the outputs of the devices that are left are ranked alike
  (DRMOutputChoice.h) and the device with the best one is opened, by
  the loop of before, which now runs for that device only;
- FindConnector() takes the best output of the device by the same rank,
  so the device that is chosen and the output that is then found on it
  cannot differ;
- of outputs that rank the same, the first: the order of the devices
  and, within a device, of its driver, as before.

With one device nothing is asked beforehand and it is opened as it
always was. So is each of several when none of them has an output that
can be used, which is what the retry of a cold boot relies on.

The panel of the machine itself (eDP, LVDS, DSI, DPI) and a
head-mounted display now come after any other output, also within one
device: a laptop with a monitor on DisplayPort or USB-C shows Kodi on
the monitor. The panel is used when it is the only display, or when it
is the one named in the settings. This is the one change for a machine
with one graphics device.

The "Monitor" setting offered the connected outputs of the device in
use. It now offers those of the other devices after them, as they were
found when the device was chosen (no device is opened to fill that
list, which is asked whenever the settings are shown). Where two
devices have a connected output of the same name, each is offered with
its device: the driver ("HDMI-A-1 (nvidia-drm)") and, for two devices
with the same driver, the driver and the address on the bus. That name
chooses it. A name saved before still means the first output of that
name. As before, a change of that setting takes effect when Kodi is
started again.

With several devices the log tells, without debug logging, what each of
them has connected, whether it is on, why a device is not for Kodi, and
which device is used.

What does not change: an output has to be on to be used.

The decision is covered by a host test (drmoutput_test.cpp), which also
compares it with what was chosen before for every device of up to three
outputs. Reading the devices cannot be run without displays: validated
in hardware only.
